&lt;p>The center will build open infrastructure, care models, and AI methods to support patients at the &amp;ldquo;edge&amp;rdquo; of medicine: everyday life, where people live, work, and make decisions 24/7.
By meeting patients there, edge medicine will bring care to where health is actually shaped.
Its foundation is &lt;a href="https://2i2c.org/collaborators/jupyterhealth/" >JupyterHealth&lt;/a>, an open source platform built on &lt;a href="https://jupyter.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" >Project Jupyter&lt;/a> that combines wearable and medical record data for patients, researchers, and care teams.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>We&amp;rsquo;ve been working with the JupyterHealth team over the past year, assisting with their open source strategy and product development. You can learn more about the open source pieces of the project at the &lt;a href="https://docs.jupyterhealth.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" >JupyterHealth documentation&lt;/a>.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>We&amp;rsquo;re excited to see Jupyter&amp;rsquo;s open approach used to improve patient care, and we look forward to supporting the work ahead!&lt;/p>
